# The Art of Thinking Clearly - Rolf Dobelli

## Overview
A comprehensive catalog of roughly 100 cognitive biases and mental errors that distort human thinking. The book organizes biases into categories such as Tendency, Decisions, Events, Others, Information, Outcomes, Judgments, and Success. Each bias is presented as a named chapter with illustrative examples and historical anecdotes.

## Key Concepts
- [[Survivorship bias]] - Focusing on winners while ignoring losers
- [[Clustering illusion]] - Seeing patterns in random data
- [[Story bias]] - Preferring coherent narratives over statistical truth
- [[Chauffeur knowledge]] - Superficial knowledge that mimics understanding
- [[Hedonic treadmill]] - Adaptation returning to baseline happiness
- [[Planning fallacy]] - Underestimating time and resources
- [[Hindsight bias]] - Overestimating ability to have predicted past events
- [[Confirmation bias]] - Seeking confirming information
- [[Groupthink]] - Collective decision failures
- [[Paradox of choice]] - Choice overload causing paralysis
- [[Anchoring]] - Initial information unduly influencing judgments
- [[Loss aversion]] - Stronger preference for avoiding losses
- [[Outcome bias]] - Judging decisions by outcomes
- [[Illusion of control]] - Believing we can influence chance events
- [[Deformation professionnelle]] - Professional training creating blind spots
- [[Zeigarnik effect]] - Incomplete tasks staying in mind
- [[Hot-hand fallacy]] - Believing success streaks continue
- [[Blind spot bias]] - Inability to recognize own biases
- [[Default effect]] - Choosing default options
- [[Illusion of validity]] - Overconfidence in predictions
- [[Volunteer's folly]] - Volunteering time while ignoring opportunity cost
- [[Strategic misrepresentation]] - Deliberately misrepresenting information
- [[Search satisficing]] - Settling for adequate rather than optimal
- [[Restraint bias]] - Overestimating ability to control impulses
- [[Relative deprivation]] - Satisfaction depending on comparisons
- [[Effort justification]] - Rationalizing effort by valuing outcomes
- [[Twaddle tendency]] - Preference for complex over simple explanations
- [[Will Rogers phenomenon]] - Definitions creating misleading statistical improvements
- [[Public goods dilemma]] - Individual rationality leading to collective irrationality
- [[Just-world hypothesis]] - Believing the world is fundamentally fair
- [[Neoophobia]] - Fear of the new hindering innovation
- [[Not-invented-here syndrome]] - Resistance to external ideas
- [[Fallacy of the single cause]] - Oversimplifying complex events
- [[Fallacy of the single decision]] - Treating life as isolated choices
- [[Personification]] - Attributing human traits to non-human entities
- [[Monetary illusion]] - Nominal prices affecting decisions
- [[Illusion of skill]] - Overestimating skill in chance-heavy domains
- [[Affect heuristic]] - Emotions influencing risk assessments
- [[Salience effect]] - Focusing on what's noticeable, not important
- [[House money effect]] - Taking greater risks with windfalls
- [[Hedonic treadmill]] - Adaptation to circumstances
- [[Expert illusion]] - Experts overestimating their knowledge
- [[Tunnel vision]] - Focus blinding to alternatives
- [[Decision fatigue]] - Depleted mental resources from many decisions
- [[Priming]] - Environmental cues influencing behavior
- [[Placebo effect]] - Beliefs creating real physiological changes
- [[Coincidence]] - Finding meaning in random coincidences
- [[Boredom]] - Role in creativity and negative aspects
- [[Procrastination]] - Delaying tasks and overcoming it
- [[Sleep]] - Sleep deprivation affecting cognitive performance
- [[Group polarization]] - Groups tending toward extreme positions
- [[False consensus effect]] - Overestimating shared views
- [[Liking bias]] - Similarity affecting judgments
- [[Envy]] - Corrosive effects on happiness
- [[Intuition]] - When to trust and doubt intuitive judgments
- [[It'll-get-worse-before-it-gets-better fallacy]] - Believing temporary suffering is necessary
- [[Forecast illusion]] - Expert predictions failing
- [[Conjunction fallacy]] - Specific scenarios judged more likely
- [[Action bias]] - Preference for action over inaction
- [[Omission commission bias]] - Harsh judgments of harmful actions
- [[Motivation crowding]] - External rewards undermining intrinsic motivation
- [[Induction]] - Limits of inductive reasoning
- [[Attribution error]] - Attributing others' behavior to personality
- [[Self-serving bias]] - Attributing success to self, failure to external
- [[Illusion of control]] - Believing we influence chance events
- [[Halo effect]] - One trait influencing overall perception
- [[Alternative paths]] - Counterfactuals avoiding attribution errors
- [[Lasting effect]] - Overestimating emotional impact of future events
- [[Probabilistic illusion]] - Poor intuitive understanding of probability
- [[Gambler's fallacy]] - Past random events influencing future
- [[Scarcity error]] - Perceived scarcity increasing value
- [[Base-rate neglect]] - Failing to consider general prevalence
- [[Social proof]] - Looking to others for correct behavior
- [[Sunk cost fallacy]] - Continuing due to past investments
- [[Reciprocity]] - Returning favors being exploited
- [[Authority bias]] - Over-reliance on authority figures
- [[Availability bias]] - Overestimating easily recalled events
- [[Contrast effect]] - Perceiving differences relative to context
- [[Expectation]] - Expectations shaping experiences
- [[Simple logic]] - Power of clear thinking
- [[Winner's curse]] - Winner in bidding overpaying
- [[Information bias]] - Seeking more information when unnecessary
- [[Contagion bias]] - Overestimating spread of ideas
- [[Problem with averages]] - Averages hiding important variations
- [[Twaddle tendency]] - Preference for complex explanations
- [[Endowment effect]] - Overvaluing owned things
- [[False causality]] - Mistaking correlation for causation
- [[In-group out-group bias]] - Favoring own group
- [[Neglect of probability]] - Ignoring probability in decisions
- [[False consensus effect]] - Overestimating shared views
- [[Strangers like me]] - Fundamental attribution error
- [[Groupthink]] - Group cohesion leading to poor decisions
- [[Forecast illusion]] - Expert predictions failing

## Tools
- [[Bias checklist]] - Systematically run through common cognitive biases
- [[Pre-mortem analysis]] - Imagine failure and determine causes
- [[Devil's advocate perspective]] - Actively argue against your position
- [[Base rate awareness]] - Start with general probabilities
- [[Time-delay decisions]] - Cooling-off periods for emotional choices
- [[Consider-the-opposite exercise]] - Actively disconfirm your hypothesis
- [[Reference class forecasting]] - Compare to similar past cases
- [[Blind spot recognition]] - Acknowledge inability to see own biases

## Mental Models
- [[Tendency]] - Pattern recognition and universal mental model
- [[Decisions]] - Decision-making as mental model
- [[Events]] - Events as mental model
- [[Others]] - Others as mental model
- [[Information]] - Information as mental model
- [[Outcomes]] - Outcomes as mental model
- [[Judgments]] - Judgments as mental model
- [[Success]] - Success as mental model

## When to Stop
- When analysis paralysis prevents necessary action
- When debiasing becomes more costly than the bias itself
- When fast, intuitive decisions are required and time is critical
- When relationships and social contexts require going with the flow
- When over-correcting and missing opportunities through excessive caution
- When acknowledging uncertainty is more valuable than false precision
- When cognitive load of debiasing exceeds decision stakes
- When developing analysis anxiety and fear of making any decision

## Key Insights
Human cognition is riddled with systematic biases that distort perception, judgment, and decision-making. Awareness and structured countermeasures can improve thinking. The book emphasizes that debiasing should not override situations requiring quick action, and that analysis paralysis is itself a risk.

